diagnosis_text:
No active network fault is present. End-to-end reachability from the user’s PC (host_1_1_1_1) to the data center is working normally. Evidence: host_1_1_1_1 can ping the DNS server (10.200.0.2) and all web servers (10.200.0.3–.6) with 0% loss and ~1.2–1.6 ms RTT. HTTP from host_1_1_1_1 to web_server_0 succeeds with sub-4 ms total time. Control-plane routing is also healthy: on switch_dist_1_1, OSPF installs routes to 10.200.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 (eth0), and 10.1.1.0/24 is directly connected on br0. On switch_server_access, 10.200.0.0/24 is directly connected and return routes to 10.1.1.0/24 are via 172.16.0.10 (eth5). Router_core_1 has OSPF routes for 10.1.1.0/24 via switch_dist_1_1, and for 10.200.0.0/24 via router_core_3, indicating all interconnects are up. 

Mechanistically, both data plane (ICMP/HTTP) and control plane (OSPF adjacency and routes) align with a fully functional path; thus the reported “lost data center” condition is not reproducible now. Localization: the building uplink (switch_dist_1_1 eth0 to core) is up and exchanging OSPF; the server-access uplink (switch_server_access eth5 to core) is also up.

Root cause: Not observed; likely transient or non-network (timing/application) issue at the time of the ticket.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Recommend monitoring OSPF neighbor stability and link events; if the issue recurs, capture timestamps and run traceroute from host_1_1_1_1 during the incident to pinpoint any intermittent link or adjacency flaps. 
